WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.227
A table orders ten dishes on one ticket.

2
00:00:05.227 --> 00:00:08.032
Each dish has a small recipe card of its own.

3
00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:17.196
At the pass, the counter where orders come in, the head cook reads the ticket one line at a time, and hands each dish to a helper at a free burner.

4
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.270
The stove has four burners, so four dishes cook at once.

5
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.385
If every dish takes 100 milliseconds, ten dishes take three rounds: four, four and two.

6
00:00:27.385 --> 00:00:29.001
About 300 milliseconds.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.557
Kotlin has an operator for exactly this kitchen, called flatMapMerge.

8
00:00:33.557 --> 00:00:36.651
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:36.651 --> 00:00:41.892
If I give it four burners, doesn't everything I write inside it run four at a time?

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.315
Let's see.

11
00:00:43.315 --> 00:00:48.535
A flow is a recipe card for values: nothing cooks until a collector asks.

12
00:00:48.535 --> 00:00:51.395
Here the ticket is a flow of dish names.

13
00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:58.545
stove.cook is a suspend call: it pauses its coroutine for 100 milliseconds without holding a thread.

14
00:00:58.545 --> 00:01:04.408
A helper wrote this: for each dish, cook it, then return a flow of that one plate.

15
00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:06.195
Four burners, ten dishes.

16
00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:06.839
How long?

17
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:11.132
1,000 milliseconds.

18
00:01:11.132 --> 00:01:16.259
One dish on the stove at a time, more than three times as long as we planned.

19
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.148
Picture it.

20
00:01:17.148 --> 00:01:21.295
Four helpers at four warm burners, each holding an empty pan.

21
00:01:21.295 --> 00:01:23.199
The calmest kitchen in town.

22
00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:25.987
So the cap of four was never the problem.

23
00:01:25.987 --> 00:01:28.231
Something before the burners was.

24
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.032
Watch the head cook.

25
00:01:30.032 --> 00:01:35.821
She reads one line, does whatever the lambda says, and only then hands the result to a burner.

26
00:01:35.821 --> 00:01:37.791
This lambda says: cook the dish.

27
00:01:37.791 --> 00:01:41.179
So she cooks it herself, alone, while the helpers wait.

28
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:42.768
Let's map it.

29
00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:46.426
The ticket is the outer flow, and each line is one value.

30
00:01:46.426 --> 00:01:51.432
The flow the lambda returns is that dish's recipe card, called the inner flow.

31
00:01:51.432 --> 00:01:54.833
The burners are lanes, and concurrency says how many.

32
00:01:54.833 --> 00:02:01.700
And the head cook is the one coroutine that reads the outer flow and runs your lambda, one value at a time.

33
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.795
We can build the operator ourselves.

34
00:02:04.795 --> 00:02:06.869
One coroutine collects the ticket.

35
00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:09.796
For each dish, it calls your lambda, right here.

36
00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:17.174
Then it waits for a free burner: a semaphore, a counter of permits, where acquire takes one and waits while none is left.

37
00:02:17.174 --> 00:02:21.748
Then it launches a lane that collects the inner flow into one shared hatch.

38
00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:24.761
The tricky line is the first one in the loop.

39
00:02:24.761 --> 00:02:29.975
The lambda runs inside the one reading coroutine, before it even asks for a burner.

40
00:02:29.975 --> 00:02:32.300
Only the flow it returns gets a lane.

41
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.001
So the slow call must live inside the returned flow: flow, emit, stove.cook.

42
00:02:38.001 --> 00:02:43.134
Now the head cook only writes the recipe card, and the helpers do the cooking.

43
00:02:43.134 --> 00:02:49.255
Reactor's flatMap in Java has the same trap: block in the mapper, and it cooks one at a time.

44
00:02:49.933 --> 00:02:53.965
Let's shrink the order to five dishes on two burners.

45
00:02:53.965 --> 00:02:59.441
Soup takes 300 milliseconds, salad 100, roast 200, bread 50 and pie 150.

46
00:02:59.441 --> 00:03:05.527
The lesson runs it on a fake clock, so the numbers come out the same every time.

47
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:09.704
With the fix, the lambda runs for soup, salad and roast at 0.

48
00:03:09.704 --> 00:03:11.888
So when does roast start cooking?

49
00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:17.605
At 100, when the salad frees a burner.

50
00:03:17.605 --> 00:03:22.526
At 300, soup and roast are done, and bread and pie take their burners.

51
00:03:22.526 --> 00:03:28.009
The order is ready at 450, with two dishes on the stove at the busiest moment.

52
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.299
And the plates leave in the order they're ready: salad, soup, roast, bread, pie.

53
00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:35.275
That's called completion order.

54
00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:37.989
Now put the cooking back in the lambda.

55
00:03:37.989 --> 00:03:40.383
800 milliseconds, one dish at a time.

56
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:44.522
That's exactly the sum of all five, what a plain map would take.

57
00:03:44.522 --> 00:03:46.075
The burners did nothing.

58
00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:47.535
So here's the rule.

59
00:03:47.535 --> 00:03:50.071
The lambda only builds the inner flow.

60
00:03:50.071 --> 00:03:51.940
Put the slow work inside it.

61
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:55.586
flatMapMerge runs the inner flows side by side.

62
00:03:55.586 --> 00:04:01.109
That's one of three ways to run them, and the other two make different promises about time.

63
00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:03.709
A table keeps changing its mind.

64
00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.221
It sends three orders, 100 milliseconds apart.

65
00:04:07.221 --> 00:04:14.855
Each order opens a recipe in two parts: part a is ready after 50 milliseconds, and part b 150 later.

66
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.054
flatMapConcat cooks one order at a time.

67
00:04:18.054 --> 00:04:19.979
So when does order 2 come in?

68
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.755
Most people say 100.

69
00:04:24.755 --> 00:04:28.091
It's 300, and the whole run takes 800.

70
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:31.693
flatMapConcat has no second coroutine and no shelf.

71
00:04:31.693 --> 00:04:34.783
Each recipe runs inside the outer flow's emit.

72
00:04:34.783 --> 00:04:41.769
Remember the cook from the first lesson, who carries a plate over and stands there while the diner eats?

73
00:04:41.769 --> 00:04:42.441
Same here.

74
00:04:42.441 --> 00:04:47.681
The waiter who brings the orders stands at the pass until both parts are done.

75
00:04:47.681 --> 00:04:50.771
Only then does his 100 millisecond wait begin.

76
00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:54.271
And here the kitchen picture breaks, in a useful way.

77
00:04:54.271 --> 00:04:58.301
A real waiter would walk back to the table while the dish cooks.

78
00:04:58.301 --> 00:05:02.395
This one can't, because even his own waiting happens inside emit.

79
00:05:02.395 --> 00:05:07.621
Put buffer in front, and he leaves the orders on a shelf instead: 600 milliseconds.

80
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.755
flatMapMerge gives each order its own burner.

81
00:05:11.755 --> 00:05:14.655
Orders come in at 0, 100 and 200.

82
00:05:14.655 --> 00:05:20.895
Parts come out as they're ready, so 1b, at 200, comes after 2a, at 150.

83
00:05:20.895 --> 00:05:21.949
Done at 400.

84
00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:24.863
flatMapLatest keeps only the newest.

85
00:05:24.863 --> 00:05:31.460
Each new order cancels the recipe in flight, waits for it to stop, and starts the next.

86
00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.431
So 1b and 2b never arrive.

87
00:05:33.431 --> 00:05:34.341
Done at 400.

88
00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:39.983
The table changed its mind twice, and the kitchen binned two half made dishes without a word.

89
00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:42.034
Every waiter has served that table.

90
00:05:42.034 --> 00:05:49.828
And it's exactly the shape you want for a search box that searches as you type, where each new letter makes the old search worthless.

91
00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:53.256
So, three operators, three promises.

92
00:05:53.256 --> 00:05:57.459
flatMapConcat: one at a time, in order, and the outer waits.

93
00:05:57.459 --> 00:06:00.822
flatMapMerge: side by side, in completion order.

94
00:06:00.822 --> 00:06:05.096
flatMapLatest: only the newest, and the old one is cancelled.

95
00:06:05.267 --> 00:06:06.062
A few rules.

96
00:06:06.062 --> 00:06:09.705
Set the number of burners from what the stove can take.

97
00:06:09.705 --> 00:06:13.613
Without it you get 16, and Reactor's flatMap gives you 256.

98
00:06:13.613 --> 00:06:18.580
Keep a cancelled recipe's cleanup short, because the next one waits for it.

99
00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:27.721
And if each dish gives one plate, and one after another is fine, you don't need a flatMap at all: a suspend call in map is easier to read.

100
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.048
One limit.

101
00:06:29.048 --> 00:06:33.778
A burnt dish in any lane cancels all the others, and the whole flow ends.

102
00:06:33.778 --> 00:06:36.953
Choosing what a failure costs is the next lesson.

103
00:06:37.733 --> 00:06:41.703
So, does everything inside flatMapMerge run four at a time?

104
00:06:41.703 --> 00:06:41.905
No.

105
00:06:41.905 --> 00:06:48.433
The head cook runs your lambda, one dish at a time, and only the recipe it returns gets a burner.

106
00:06:48.433 --> 00:06:53.547
Put the cooking there, and the ten dish ticket is ready at 300 milliseconds.

107
00:06:53.733 --> 00:06:54.630
Quick check.

108
00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:59.560
The outer emits a value, then waits 100 milliseconds, three times.

109
00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.324
Each inner gives one value after 200.

110
00:07:02.324 --> 00:07:04.789
How long does flatMapConcat take?

111
00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:14.717
900 milliseconds: three times 200 plus 100, because the outer waits inside emit.

112
00:07:14.717 --> 00:07:16.965
With buffer in front, 600.
